                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Multiplies every element of one array by every element of another, giving a table: row i, column j holds in 1's value i times in 2's value j.\n\nTwo lists of 12 and 16 values give a 12 x 16 array. Nothing is added up - that is the difference from the dot or inner product, which collapse the same multiplications into one number. Every row of the result is in 2 scaled by one value of in 1, and every column is in 1 scaled by one value of in 2.\n\nReach for it to build a 2-D picture from two 1-D profiles - a vignette from a horizontal and a vertical fall-off, a gradient, a separable filter kernel - or a table of every pairing of two sets of weights. Any outer product has rank 1 (see np.linalg.matrix_rank): however detailed it looks, it is made of one row pattern.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.outer\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe values for the rows (a list is turned into an array). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\nin 2:\nThe values for the columns. It is only stored, so it must arrive BEFORE in 1; nothing is sent until it has.\nArrays of more than one dimension are flattened first, so the result is always 2-D: (length of in 1) x (length of in 2). The two need not be the same length.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nouter_product:\nThe 2-D table of products.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nin 1 is a ramp of 12 values from -1 to 1 (drag start and stop on its np.linspace - a metro re-sends it); in 2 is a hump of 16 values, rising from 0 to 1 and back (sin of 0 to 180 degrees, made once in make_hump). The picture is the outer product, 12 rows by 16 columns: every row is the hump, scaled by that row's ramp value - red (negative) at the top, pale through the middle row, blue at the bottom. Drag stop to -1 and the picture turns over; set start and stop both to 1 and every row is the same hump.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.inner and np.dot multiply AND add up, giving a number. np.matmul of a column (12 x 1) and a row (1 x 16) gives the same table. np.linalg.matrix_rank of any outer product is 1.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 876,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Multiplies every element of one array by every element of another, giving a table: row i, column j holds in 1's value i times in 2's value j.\n\nTwo lists of 12 and 16 values give a 12 x 16 array. Nothing is added up - that is the difference from the dot or inner product, which collapse the same multiplications into one number. Every row of the result is in 2 scaled by one value of in 1, and every column is in 1 scaled by one value of in 2.\n\nReach for it to build a 2-D picture from two 1-D profiles - a vignette from a horizontal and a vertical fall-off, a gradient, a separable filter kernel - or a table of every pairing of two sets of weights. Any outer product has rank 1 (see np.linalg.matrix_rank): however detailed it looks, it is made of one row pattern.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.outer\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe values for the rows (a list is turned into an array). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\nin 2:\nThe values for the columns. It is only stored, so it must arrive BEFORE in 1; nothing is sent until it has.\nArrays of more than one dimension are flattened first, so the result is always 2-D: (length of in 1) x (length of in 2). The two need not be the same length.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nouter_product:\nThe 2-D table of products.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nin 1 is a ramp of 12 values from -1 to 1 (drag start and stop on its np.linspace - a metro re-sends it); in 2 is a hump of 16 values, rising from 0 to 1 and back (sin of 0 to 180 degrees, made once in make_hump). The picture is the outer product, 12 rows by 16 columns: every row is the hump, scaled by that row's ramp value - red (negative) at the top, pale through the middle row, blue at the bottom. Drag stop to -1 and the picture turns over; set start and stop both to 1 and every row is the same hump.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.inner and np.dot multiply AND add up, giving a number. np.matmul of a column (12 x 1) and a row (1 x 16) gives the same table. np.linalg.matrix_rank of any outer product is 1."
                },
